---
layout: article
title: Development workflow
description: Move Appwrite changes from a development environment to staging and production with one CLI config, one API key per environment, and a CI pipeline.
difficulty: intermediate
readtime: 8
---

Environments let one Appwrite project carry a release from development to production. This guide sets up a common flow:

- A dev environment, where schema changes, functions, and sites are built and tried.
- A staging environment, which gets every change merged to your main branch, so it can be checked against a production-shaped backend.
- Production, the project itself, which gets a change when you publish a release.

The same CLI config file describes the backend for all three. Only the environment ID and the API key change between them.

# Create the environments {% #create %}

[Create two environments](/docs/advanced/environments/create) in your project: `dev` and `staging`. Choose **Clone of the project** for both, so they start with production's databases, buckets, functions, and settings.

Then create an API key inside each environment, and one in production. Switch to the environment in the Console, open **API Keys**, and create a key with the scopes your deploy needs, such as `tables.write`, `buckets.write`, and `functions.write`. Each key acts only in the environment it was created in.

# Link the CLI to the development environment {% #link %}

In your repository, link the project with the `dev` environment:

```sh
appwrite init project --project-id "<PROJECT_ID>" --environment-id dev
```

Pull the backend into `appwrite.config.json`:

```sh
appwrite pull all
```

Commit `appwrite.config.json`. It holds the project ID, the endpoint, and the definitions of your tables, buckets, functions, and sites. Build in `dev` from here: change the config, then push it.

```sh
appwrite push all
```

When a change works in `dev`, open a pull request with the updated config.

{% info title="Keep the environment ID out of the config in CI" %}
`appwrite init project` saves `environmentId` in `appwrite.config.json`. CI jobs set `APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID`, which takes precedence over the config file, so each job picks its own environment whatever the file says. Remove `environmentId` from the committed file if you don't want local commands to default to `dev`.
{% /info %}

# Deploy to staging and production from CI {% #ci %}

The pipeline runs the same commands for both targets. Only three values change:

| Value | Staging | Production |
|---|---|---|
| `APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID` | Your project ID | Your project ID |
| `APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID` | `staging` | Not set |
| API key | A key created in `staging` | A key created in production |

With GitHub Actions, create two [GitHub environments](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/deployment/targeting-different-environments/using-environments-for-deployment), `staging` and `production`. Add an `APPWRITE_API_KEY` secret to each with its key, and an `APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID` variable set to `staging` in the `staging` environment only.

Then add a workflow that deploys to staging on every push to `main`, and to production when a release is published:

```yaml
name: Deploy Appwrite

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  release:
    types: [published]

jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    environment: ${{ github.event_name == 'release' && 'production' || 'staging' }}
    env:
      APPWRITE_PROJECT_ID: <PROJECT_ID>
      APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID: ${{ vars.APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID }}
      APPWRITE_REASON: Deploy ${{ github.sha }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Install the Appwrite CLI
        run: npm install -g appwrite-cli

      - name: Configure the CLI
        run: |
          appwrite client \
            --endpoint https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1 \
            --key "${{ secrets.APPWRITE_API_KEY }}"

      - name: Deploy
        run: appwrite push all --all --force
```

When `APPWRITE_ENVIRONMENT_ID` is empty, the CLI acts on the project itself, so the production job needs no special case. `APPWRITE_REASON` records the commit next to every change in the environment's **Activity** tab. [Learn more about the CLI in CI](/docs/tooling/command-line/non-interactive).

{% info title="Settings are pushed to production separately" %}
`appwrite push all` skips project settings in an environment. Change auth methods, SMTP, and other project settings in each environment from the Console, or push them to production from a job without an environment ID.
{% /info %}

# Point your app at each environment {% #app %}

Give each build of your app the same endpoint and project ID, and the environment ID of its target. A preview deployment of your frontend can use `staging`, and the production build leaves the environment ID out. See [Connect to an environment](/docs/advanced/environments/connect#sdks).

# Start staging over {% #reset %}

A copy is a one-time snapshot. When `staging` drifts too far from production, [delete it](/docs/advanced/environments/manage#delete) and create it again as a clone of the project, then create a new API key in it and update the `staging` secret in CI.
